{
  "version": 1,
  "cells": [
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "This notebook is a quickstart tutorial for TypeScript using `.jsnb` notebook developed in JS/TS Notebook Extension in vscode. \r\n\r\nTo execute the code, use the JS/TS Kernel in vscode. ",
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "// Basic TypeScript example: Variables and types\r\nlet message: string = \"Hello, TypeScript!\";\r\nlet count: number = 42;\r\nlet isActive: boolean = true;\r\n\r\nconsole.log(message, count, isActive);",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "mime": "application/vnd.code.notebook.stdout",
          "data": "Hello, TypeScript! 42 true"
        },
        {
          "mime": "application/vnd.jsnb.timing+json",
          "data": "{\n\t\"elapsedMs\": 11\n}"
        }
      ],
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## What is TypeScript?\n\nTypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds static typing. It helps catch errors early and improves code maintainability. TypeScript code compiles to plain JavaScript that runs in any browser or Node.js environment.",
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## Functions in TypeScript\n\nFunctions can have typed parameters and return types. This helps prevent bugs.",
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "// Function example with types\nfunction greet(name: string): string {\n    return `Hello, ${name}!`;\n}\n\nfunction add(a: number, b: number): number {\n    return a + b;\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet(\"World\"));\nconsole.log(add(5, 3));",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "mime": "application/vnd.code.notebook.stdout",
          "data": "Hello, World!"
        },
        {
          "mime": "application/vnd.code.notebook.stdout",
          "data": "8"
        },
        {
          "mime": "application/vnd.jsnb.timing+json",
          "data": "{\n\t\"elapsedMs\": 17\n}"
        }
      ],
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## Interfaces and Objects\n\nTypeScript allows defining interfaces to describe object shapes.",
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "// Interface example\ninterface Person {\n    name: string;\n    age: number;\n}\n\nfunction introduce(person: Person): string {\n    return `Hi, I'm ${person.name} and I'm ${person.age} years old.`;\n}\n\nconst john: Person = { name: \"John\", age: 30 };\nconsole.log(introduce(john));",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "mime": "application/vnd.code.notebook.stdout",
          "data": "Hi, I'm John and I'm 30 years old."
        },
        {
          "mime": "application/vnd.jsnb.timing+json",
          "data": "{\n\t\"elapsedMs\": 10\n}"
        }
      ],
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## Running TypeScript\n\nIn this .jsnb notebook, you can execute TypeScript cells directly. For standalone projects, install TypeScript globally with `npm install -g typescript` and compile with `tsc filename.ts`.\n\nTo run the cells above, click the play button next to each code cell. The output will appear below.",
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ],
  "metadata": {}
}